strict inequality and gives a scale no larger than δ0. These are implications for arbitrary positive L,δ0,δ. Discharge L1 and universally quantify k,A,s with its positivity condition, obtaining the exported conclusion.\"}, {\"id\": \"S004\", \"text\": \"H002 and M012 imply that E is bounded in the Euclidean metric bornology. In Euclidean space, a bounded set has its distances from the origin bounded by a finite constant; increasing that bound to a positive number bounds each absolute coordinate of every x∈E, because for every coordinate j<n, |x_j|≤(∑_{j<n}|x_j|^2)^(1/2). Thus there exists R>0 such that E⊆[−R,R]^n. This interpretation uses precisely the metric bornology, whose bounded sets lie in some finite-radius ball.\"}, {\"id\": \"S005\", \"text\": \"Choose R from F001. For arbitrary s>0,δ>0, cover E by the open balls of radius δ/2 centered at every point of Eucl(n). They cover since each point belongs to its own ball. Each ball is open in D001's metric topology, and its diameter is≤δ by the triangle inequality. H002 and M013 supply a finite subcover. Thus a finite ball-cover with these diameters exists for every positive δ.\"}, {\"id\": \"S006\", \"text\": \"For each positive s and δ in F002, choose its finite subcover T. Each ball's s-cost is≤δ^s, so the finite cost sum is finite. The infimum H^s_δ(E) is no greater than that cost, and is therefore finite. This proves the universally quantified finite-cover and content clauses in the exported conclusion.\"}, {\"id\": \"S007\", \"text\": \"First assume L1: n≥1. For 0<h≤1 partition each coordinate interval [−R,R] into at most ceil(2R/h) closed subintervals of length≤h. Their products cover the box and hence E. The number of cubes (possibly shorter at the boundary) is≤(2R/h+1)^n≤C h^(−n), where C=(2R+1)^n depends only on the fixed box. Each diameter is≤√n h by summing the squared coordinate differences. For s>n the total s-cost is≤C n^(s/2) h^(s−n), which tends to zero as h↓0 by M015 with y=s−n>0 and source variable 1/h; the factor C n^(s/2) is fixed and finite. For any fixed target scale, h can be arbitrarily small while √n h is below it; hence every scale content is zero and H^s(E)=0. N001 gives D≤s for every s>n. It follows that D≤n: if D>n, choose a finite real s strictly between n and D (also possible if D=∞), contradicting D≤s. Discharge L1. In the remaining case L2: n=0, Eucl(0) has only the empty vector; H001 implies E=Eucl(0). Its singleton cover has zero s-cost for every s>0, so N001 gives D≤s for every positive s, whence D=0. Discharge L2. Nonnegativity in both cases is N001, giving 0≤D≤n and all the remaining clauses of the exported conclusion.\"}, {\"id\": \"S008\", \"text\": \"Fix B,ρ,η and assume L1: B is nonempty, ρ≥0, every pair in B has distance≤ρ, and η>0. For any b0∈B the pairwise bound puts B⊆[b0−ρ,b0+ρ]; since B is nonempty, such a center exists and proves both boundedness conditions. M016 and M017 therefore give a real least upper bound and a real greatest lower bound of B. Each is unique by order antisymmetry; denote them sup B and inf B. For every b'∈B, all b∈B satisfy b≤b'+ρ; the least-upper-bound property gives sup B≤b'+ρ. Thus sup B−ρ is a lower bound for B, so the greatest-lower-bound property gives sup B−ρ≤inf B. Therefore sup B−inf B≤ρ, including ρ=0.\"}, {\"id\": \"S009\", \"text\": \"Define J=(inf B−η/2,sup B+η/2) using the endpoints established in F001. Every element of B lies between inf B and sup B, and η>0 makes both endpoint containments strict. The interval is nonempty, open and bounded. Its length is sup B−inf B+η≤ρ+η by F001. Discharge L1 and quantify B,ρ,η to obtain precisely the exported conclusion. This argument asserts no measurability of B.\"}, {\"id\": \"S010\", \"text\": \"In the case L1: n=0, N002 gives E=Eucl(0), a singleton, and D=0. The coordinate map t↦((),t) preserves Euclidean distances by D001. For any a>1, the N equal closed pieces of I therefore give a cover of F of maximum diameter 1/N and total a-cost N(1/N)^a=N^(1−a)→0 as N→∞ by M015 with y=a−1>0 (restricting the real variable to positive integers). For any fixed scale the same covers eventually have smaller diameter, so H^a_δ(F)=0 at every δ>0 and H^a(F)=0. N001 implies dim_H(F)≤a for all a>1 and hence dim_H(F)≤1=D+1. Discharge L1; this also proves the more precise zero-measure assertion for n=0, since q>D=0 gives a=q+1>1.\"}, {\"id\": \"S011\", \"text\": \"Assume L2: n≥1, q>D, 0<r≤1, and ε>0. N002 gives D≥0, so q>0. N001 gives H^q(E)=0 and an r-cover U0 of q-cost<ε/2. Discard empty members. Each zero-diameter nonempty member is a singleton, since all its pairwise distances vanish. Replace its member at original index i by an open ball about that point of positive diameter≤r and q-cost<ε 2^(−i−2). Such balls exist in Eucl(n) because n≥1: a radius smaller than both r/2 and (ε 2^(−i−2))^(1/q)/2 works, and the ball has positive diameter. Leave positive-diameter members unchanged. Countable choice makes these replacements simultaneously. The added cost is bounded by ∑_{i≥0} ε 2^(−i−2)=ε/2, so the resulting cover has positive diameters≤r and cost<ε. Discharge L2 and quantify q,r,ε to establish the indicated existence statement.\"}, {\"id\": \"S012\", \"text\": \"Assume L3: n≥1, q>D, 0<r≤1 and ε>0, and choose U from F002. Define r_i=diam(U_i), N_i=ceil(1/r_i), and W_(i,j)=U_i×[(j−1)/N_i,j/N_i] for 1≤j≤N_i. Since 0<r_i≤1, N_i≥1, 1/N_i≤r_i and N_i≤1/r_i+1≤2/r_i. The W form a countable cover of F: first cover the E coordinate by U_i, then partition I in the corresponding i-th fiber. Two points of the same W differ by≤r_i horizontally and≤r_i vertically, so their Euclidean distance is≤√2 r_i. Therefore ∑_{i,j} diam(W_(i,j))^(q+1) ≤ ∑_i N_i(√2 r_i)^(q+1) ≤2(√2)^(q+1)∑_i r_i^q <2(√2)^(q+1)ε. Regrouping is valid for nonnegative series, defined as suprema of finite sums. Discharge L3 and quantify the parameters, yielding the product-cover estimate for every admissible q,r,ε.\"}, {\"id\": \"S013\", \"text\": \"Assume L4: n≥1 and q>D. For any δ>0 define r_*=min(1,δ/√2)>0 and apply F003 with its scale argument equal to r_*. For every ε>0 the resulting cover is admissible at δ and has (q+1)-cost<2(√2)^(q+1)ε. The coefficient is positive and finite, so these costs can be smaller than any positive threshold. Nonnegativity of the infimum implies H^(q+1)_δ(F)=0. Since δ was arbitrary, taking the supremum gives H^(q+1)(F)=0. N001, with positive exponent q+1, gives dim_H(F)≤q+1. Discharge L4 and quantify q to obtain this conclusion for every q>D when n≥1.\"}, {\"id\": \"S014\", \"text\": \"By N002, D is finite. In the n≥1 case, F004 gives dim_H(F)≤q+1 for every q>D. One such q first makes dim_H(F) finite. If dim_H(F)>D+1, a q strictly between D and dim_H(F)−1 contradicts that inequality; hence dim_H(F)≤D+1. This is the limit q↓D without assuming a measure statement at the critical exponent. The other case is F001, and n is either zero or at least one. F001 and F004 supply the more precise zero statements in their respective cases. Thus the exported conclusion follows in full.\"}, {\"id\": \"S015\", \"text\": \"Fix s,δ,V and temporarily assume L1: s>0, δ>0, V covers F, and diam(V_i)≤δ for every i. Put K={i:V_i≠∅}, ρ_i=diam(V_i), and take the projections A_i and B_i as declared. The Euclidean distance formula gives dist(x,y)≤dist((x,t),(y,u)) and |t−u|≤dist((x,t),(y,u)); hence diam(A_i)≤ρ_i≤δ and the pairwise distances in the nonempty B_i are≤ρ_i. No measurability is asserted for V_i,A_i,B_i. Put c=H^s_δ(E), finite by N002. For arbitrary ε assume L2: ε>0 and set η_i=ε 2^(−i−1)/(1+ρ_i^s)>0. N003 supplies an open interval enclosing each B_i of length≤ρ_i+η_i, including when ρ_i=0. Countable choice gives a family of such intervals.\"}, {\"id\": \"S016\", \"text\": \"Choose the interval family from F004 and define g_i(t)=ρ_i^s 1_(J_i∩I)(t). For t∈I and x∈E, the point (x,t) lies in F and therefore in some V_i. That index belongs to K, and x∈A_i while t∈B_i⊆J_i. Thus the A_i with t∈J_i cover E, with diameters≤ρ_i≤δ. 